# Talisman allowlist.
#
# Every entry is a reviewed false positive. Checksums are content-based, so any
# edit to one of these files makes Talisman scan it again and the entry has to be
# re-reviewed. Nothing listed here is a real credential.
#
#   package-lock.json             base64 sha512 integrity hashes, and npm packages
#                                 whose names contain "pass" (minipass, @inquirer/password)
#   *.ts / *.css                  HTML form attributes (type="password",
#                                 autocomplete="current-password" / "new-password"),
#                                 storage key names, and a base64 data: URI for an
#                                 inline SVG
#   main/application.properties   only ${ENV_VAR} placeholders -- the real values come
#                                 from the environment and are never committed
#   test/application.properties   a test-only signing key used to sign throwaway
#                                 tokens in the suite; no deployed environment uses it
#   JwtServiceTest.java           the same test-only key, plus a second one used to
#                                 prove a token signed with a different key is rejected
#   README.md                     documentation placeholders and a Maven property
#   Java sources / mvnw           Spring type names and fields that merely contain
#                                 "password" (UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken,
#                                 passwordEncoder, MVNW_PASSWORD in the Maven wrapper)
#   .env.example                  template with EMPTY values and a comment showing
#                                 how to generate a key; the real .env is git-ignored
